-- Generated by tasks/content-cutover/scripts/build-seeds.ts from content/ at cc079bee7.
UPDATE `articles` SET `body` = `body` || '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Everything in Linux is a file ... and every Linux container''s resource limits are no different. They''re just files that live in a special directory."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Inside that directory are files with names like "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.max"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.weight"},{"type":"text","text":". When the kernel decides your container has used too much RAM, it isn''t consulting a daemon or asking Kubernetes. It''s reading a number out of a file you could have written yourself with "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"echo"},{"type":"text","text":". That''s the whole mechanism. The story of how Linux got here, and the decade-long detour it took along the way, is the story of cgroups."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"see-it-for-yourself"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"See It For Yourself"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If you''re on a Linux machine running a modern distribution, try this:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"ls /sys/fs/cgroup/"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You''ll see something like:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":null,"title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"cgroup.controllers     cpu.stat            memory.pressure\ncgroup.max.depth       cpuset.cpus         memory.stat\ncgroup.procs           io.pressure         pids.current\ncgroup.subtree_control memory.current      pids.max\ncpu.pressure           memory.max          system.slice/\n                                           user.slice/"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is a kernel pseudo-filesystem mounted under "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/sys"},{"type":"text","text":". On a cgroupsv2 host, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/sys/fs/cgroup"},{"type":"text","text":" itself is "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cgroup2fs"},{"type":"text","text":", not ordinary disk-backed storage. Reading a file there asks the kernel a question. Writing to one issues a command. The kernel responds by changing its own state. There is no daemon in the middle."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.max"},{"type":"text","text":" is the memory limit. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.pressure"},{"type":"text","text":" reports whether tasks are stalling on CPU. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"system.slice/"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"user.slice/"},{"type":"text","text":" are sub-folders: child groups, with their own limits inside."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"what-a-cgroup-actually-is"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What a Cgroup Actually Is"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A cgroup (short for \"control group\") is a Linux kernel mechanism for organizing processes into named groups and applying resource constraints to those groups. That''s the entire definition."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Membership lives in a file: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cgroup.procs"},{"type":"text","text":" contains the PIDs of processes in the group. To move a process in, you write its PID. To take it out, write that PID to another group''s "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cgroup.procs"},{"type":"text","text":". The kernel handles the bookkeeping."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The mental model is this: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"a cgroup is a directory, process membership is a file entry, and every resource limit is a knob you turn by writing to a file."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The kernel reads these files and enforces the constraints. If a process in a cgroup pushes memory usage toward "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.max"},{"type":"text","text":", the kernel first tries to reclaim pages from that cgroup; if usage crosses "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.high"},{"type":"text","text":" along the way, allocations are throttled with backpressure; if reclaim can''t keep up and usage hits "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.max"},{"type":"text","text":", the kernel OOM-kills a process inside the cgroup. No daemon, no userspace agent. It''s the kernel itself doing the enforcement."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This design is elegant. What went wrong is what always goes wrong in systems that grow organically: nobody planned for containers."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"try-it-yourself"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Try It Yourself"}]},{"type":"aside","attrs":{"variant":"info"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Tested on:"},{"type":"text","text":" Ubuntu 25.10, kernel 7.0.5, systemd 257, runc 1.3.4. Anything cgroupsv2 with these or newer should behave identically."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The cleanest way to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"feel"},{"type":"text","text":" how cgroups work is to create one, cap it, and watch the kernel enforce the cap. Do this on a disposable Linux VM or lab host, not a production node. Any cgroupsv2 system will do."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"First, make sure the kernel will expose the controllers we want in our new cgroup. On v2, a child cgroup only sees a controller if its parent has enabled it in "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cgroup.subtree_control"},{"type":"text","text":":"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Should list at least: memory pids (and probably cpu, io, cpuset)\ncat /sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.subtree_control\n\n# If memory or pids is missing, enable them at the root:\necho \"+memory +pids\" | sudo tee /sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.subtree_control"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most modern distros enable these by default, but managed nodes and minimal images sometimes don''t. With that out of the way:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Create the cgroup (it''s literally a directory)\nsudo mkdir /sys/fs/cgroup/demo\n\n# Cap memory at 50 MiB, swap at 0 (otherwise overflow spills to swap\n# on hosts where swap is enabled, masking the kill)\necho $((50 * 1024 * 1024)) | sudo tee /sys/fs/cgroup/demo/memory.max\necho 0                     | sudo tee /sys/fs/cgroup/demo/memory.swap.max\n\n# Run a memory hog inside the cgroup. The subshell joins the cgroup\n# by writing its PID to cgroup.procs, then execs python which tries\n# to allocate 200 MiB.\nsudo sh -c ''\n  echo $$ > /sys/fs/cgroup/demo/cgroup.procs\n  exec python3 -c \"x = b\\\"A\\\" * (200 * 1024 * 1024); print(\\\"survived\\\")\"\n''"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You''ll see "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"Killed"},{"type":"text","text":" rather than "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"survived"},{"type":"text","text":". The kill is scoped to the cgroup we just made. On hosts where you can read "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"dmesg"},{"type":"text","text":":"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"sudo dmesg | tail -n 20\n# ... Memory cgroup out of memory: Killed process 12345 (python3) ...\n# ... oom-kill:constraint=CONSTRAINT_MEMCG,...,oom_memcg=/demo,..."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"On hosts where "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"dmesg"},{"type":"text","text":" is restricted (managed nodes, hardened distros), read the cgroup''s own counter instead:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"cat /sys/fs/cgroup/demo/memory.events\n# low      0\n# high     0\n# max      <n>     # times memory.max was hit\n# oom      <n>     # times the cgroup went OOM\n# oom_kill <n>     # processes the kernel killed"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No daemon was involved. The kernel read "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.max"},{"type":"text","text":" (50 MiB), watched "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.current"},{"type":"text","text":" climb past it, and pulled the trigger. Clean up the cgroup once it has no live processes (an empty cgroup is the only kind "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"rmdir"},{"type":"text","text":" will remove):"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"sudo rmdir /sys/fs/cgroup/demo"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For real workloads on a systemd host, prefer "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"systemd-run"},{"type":"text","text":" with unit properties ("},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"--property=MemoryMax=50M"},{"type":"text","text":", etc.) over editing "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/sys/fs/cgroup/"},{"type":"text","text":" by hand. systemd owns the cgroup tree, and writing directly to it from outside breaks its accounting. The raw interface above is the right shape for "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"learning"},{"type":"text","text":" what the kernel is doing; it''s the wrong shape for production resource control."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Three primitives. A directory, a file write, a PID. That''s the kernel interface Linux container runtimes are built on."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"now-block-a-fork-bomb"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now Block a Fork Bomb"}]},{"type":"aside","attrs":{"variant":"danger"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Look, don''t be stupid, do not trust my code, do not say I didn''t warn you. Only do this on a disposable Linux VM or lab host you don''t mind rebooting, not a production node or your laptop. If anything goes wrong (the cgroup isn''t set up correctly, your shell isn''t actually inside it, the limit isn''t applied), the spawn loop further down has nothing to stop it and will exhaust the kernel''s process table within seconds. Recovery means a hard reset. Read the whole section before running anything, and verify "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cat /proc/self/cgroup"},{"type":"text","text":" shows "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"0::/fork-demo"},{"type":"text","text":" before you launch the loop."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The memory demo killed a process after it allocated too much. The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"pids"},{"type":"text","text":" controller is more interesting: it refuses to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"create"},{"type":"text","text":" the process at all. Same files, same write-a-PID-and-go pattern, but the kernel says no at fork time rather than reclaim time."}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# sudo doesn''t apply to shell redirection, so `sudo echo X > /sys/fs/...`\n# writes as your user and fails. Drop into a real root shell instead.\nsudo -i\n\n# Create the cgroup and cap it at 10 processes\nmkdir /sys/fs/cgroup/fork-demo\necho 10 > /sys/fs/cgroup/fork-demo/pids.max\n\n# Move the current shell into it\necho $$ > /sys/fs/cgroup/fork-demo/cgroup.procs\n\ncat /proc/self/cgroup\n# 0::/fork-demo"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now spawn background "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"sleep"},{"type":"text","text":" processes in a loop and let the counter climb to the wall:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"i=0\nwhile true; do\n  sleep 60 &\n  i=$((i+1))\n  echo \"spawned #$i → PID $!  (pids.current=$(cat /sys/fs/cgroup/fork-demo/pids.current))\"\n  sleep 0.2\ndone"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":null,"title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"spawned #1 → PID 367  (pids.current=3)\nspawned #2 → PID 370  (pids.current=4)\nspawned #3 → PID 373  (pids.current=5)\nspawned #4 → PID 376  (pids.current=6)\nspawned #5 → PID 379  (pids.current=7)\nspawned #6 → PID 382  (pids.current=8)\nspawned #7 → PID 385  (pids.current=9)\nspawned #8 → PID 388  (pids.current=10)\nbash: fork: retry: Resource temporarily unavailable\nbash: fork: retry: Resource temporarily unavailable\nbash: fork: retry: Resource temporarily unavailable"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The eighth "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"sleep"},{"type":"text","text":" pushes "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"pids.current"},{"type":"text","text":" to exactly "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"pids.max=10"},{"type":"text","text":". (The shell counts as one process, and the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cat"},{"type":"text","text":" inside the command substitution briefly shows up as another, which is why the visible count starts at 3.) Every subsequent "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"fork()"},{"type":"text","text":" returns "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"EAGAIN"},{"type":"text","text":", bash prints the failure, and the cgroup is sealed. Ctrl+C to stop."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The kernel keeps a running tally of denials:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"cat /sys/fs/cgroup/fork-demo/pids.events\n# max 4823"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That''s how many "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"fork()"},{"type":"text","text":" calls the kernel refused since the cgroup was created. Every denied "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"fork()"},{"type":"text","text":" is one the rest of the system never had to deal with. Point a real fork bomb at this cgroup and the counter climbs into the millions while the host stays responsive, but I''m not going to print the bomb here, because copy-pasting one into the wrong shell is exactly how lab hosts become bricks. The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"sleep"},{"type":"text","text":" loop above already proves the mechanism: the kernel returns "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"EAGAIN"},{"type":"text","text":" to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"fork()"},{"type":"text","text":" the moment "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"pids.current"},{"type":"text","text":" hits "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"pids.max"},{"type":"text","text":", and it will do that whether the caller is a polite shell loop or a self-replicating function."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cleanup once the loop has exited. The root shell still belongs to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"fork-demo"},{"type":"text","text":", and the kernel won''t let us "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"rmdir"},{"type":"text","text":" a cgroup with live members, so exit the shell first and remove the directory from the parent shell that spawned it:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kill $(jobs -p)   # reap the sleeps from inside the root shell\nexit              # drop out of the root shell; its PID leaves the cgroup with it\nsudo rmdir /sys/fs/cgroup/fork-demo"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two cgroups, two different "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"<controller>.max"},{"type":"text","text":" files, two completely different kinds of refusal: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.max"},{"type":"text","text":" reclaims-then-kills, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"pids.max"},{"type":"text","text":" returns "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"EAGAIN"},{"type":"text","text":" to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"fork()"},{"type":"text","text":". The kernel exposes both behaviours through identical-looking interfaces, which is exactly the point of cgroups as an abstraction."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"controllers-who-fills-the-folder"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Controllers: Who Fills the Folder"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A cgroup is a folder. But who fills it with files like "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.max"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.weight"},{"type":"text","text":"?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The kernel does, but not as a single monolithic block. Each resource is governed by a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"controller"},{"type":"text","text":": a piece of kernel code dedicated to one resource type. The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory"},{"type":"text","text":" controller watches RAM, exposes "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.max"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.current"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.pressure"},{"type":"text","text":", and enforces the limits. The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu"},{"type":"text","text":" controller weights and quota-caps CPU time. The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"io"},{"type":"text","text":" controller throttles block I/O. The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"pids"},{"type":"text","text":" controller caps the number of processes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most resource-control files in a cgroup folder belong to one controller. The naming convention makes this explicit: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"<controller>.<metric>"},{"type":"text","text":". "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.current"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.stat"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"io.max"},{"type":"text","text":". Core cgroup files use the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cgroup.*"},{"type":"text","text":" prefix instead."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The set of controllers available on your system lives in one file:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.controllers\n# cpuset cpu io memory pids"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Five controllers on this system. Today."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It used to be many controllers spread across many possible trees, and that''s how the trouble started."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"where-this-came-from"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Where This Came From"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In 2006, Paul Menage and Rohit Seth "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://lkml.org/lkml/2006/9/14/370","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"proposed adding \"process containers\""},{"type":"text","text":" to the Linux kernel: a way to group processes and attach resource controllers to those groups. The name was quickly changed to avoid confusion with the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"other"},{"type":"text","text":" kind of container that was starting to gain traction. Solaris Zones, Linux-VServer, and OpenVZ all already used \"container\" to mean a fully isolated user-space (own filesystem, network, process tree, users), and this new patch did none of that. It just grouped processes and counted their resource use."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"By the time the patch merged into "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://kernelnewbies.org/Linux_2_6_24","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"Linux 2.6.24"},{"type":"text","text":" in January 2008, it was called "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Task Control Groups"},{"type":"text","text":" (TCG). The release notes describe it as a framework that can \"track and group processes into arbitrary ''cgroups'' and assign arbitrary state to those groups, in order to control its behaviour\", with other subsystems hooking in to provide new attributes. The \"Task\" prefix quietly fell out of use over the next few years; everyone settled on the short form: control groups, or "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"cgroups"},{"type":"text","text":". From there, controllers were added one by one, year after year:"}]},{"type":"interactive","attrs":{"component":"CgroupTimeline","props":{},"hydrate":"visible"}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"By 2013, more than a dozen controllers existed. The original design had made a decision that seemed perfectly reasonable at the time: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"controllers could live in independent trees."},{"type":"text","text":" CPU could get one tree. Memory could get another. Block I/O could get another. The promise was flexibility: you could organize processes differently for different resources."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In practice, almost nobody used that flexibility. And the cost would dominate the next decade."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"hierarchies-cgroups-inside-cgroups"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hierarchies: Cgroups Inside Cgroups"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cgroups nest. A cgroup can contain child cgroups, and those children can have their own children. The result is a tree. The kernel calls it a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"hierarchy"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"interactive","attrs":{"component":"CgroupTreeDiagram","props":{},"hydrate":"visible"}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Children inherit constraints from their parents. If "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"system.slice/"},{"type":"text","text":" caps memory at 8 GiB, no combination of "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"nginx.service/"},{"type":"text","text":" plus "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"postgres.service/"},{"type":"text","text":" may exceed 8 GiB together, even if neither child sets a limit of its own. Limits cascade down; usage rolls up."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One tree, one hierarchy. Hold on to that picture."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In cgroupsv1, there wasn''t "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"one"},{"type":"text","text":" tree. There could be many."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"cgroupsv1-the-wild-west"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"cgroupsv1: The Wild West"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You''re debugging a container that keeps getting OOM killed. You SSH into the node, find the PID, and ask the kernel which cgroups the process belongs to. That information lives in a per-process file under "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/proc"},{"type":"text","text":", the other kernel pseudo-filesystem, where each running process has its own directory:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"cat /proc/1234/cgroup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"And you get... this:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":null,"title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"12:blkio:/docker/a1b2c3d4e5f6\n11:devices:/docker/a1b2c3d4e5f6\n10:memory:/docker/a1b2c3d4e5f6\n9:cpuacct:/docker/a1b2c3d4e5f6\n8:cpu:/docker/a1b2c3d4e5f6\n7:cpuset:/docker/a1b2c3d4e5f6\n6:freezer:/docker/a1b2c3d4e5f6\n5:net_cls,net_prio:/docker/a1b2c3d4e5f6\n4:perf_event:/docker/a1b2c3d4e5f6\n3:pids:/docker/a1b2c3d4e5f6\n2:hugetlb:/docker/a1b2c3d4e5f6\n1:name=systemd:/docker/a1b2c3d4e5f6"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Twelve lines. The same container ID, repeated across many hierarchies. The exact list varied by distro and runtime because related controllers could be co-mounted, but the important part is the same: one process had multiple cgroup memberships. (A few of those names won''t survive into v2 in the same form. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"freezer"},{"type":"text","text":", for instance, was v1''s way of pausing every process in a cgroup at once — useful for checkpoint/restore and as the prerequisite step to safely killing a process tree. v2 replaces it with "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cgroup.freeze"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cgroup.kill"},{"type":"text","text":" on the unified hierarchy.)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is cgroupsv1. And it''s a mess."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The process is in the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory"},{"type":"text","text":" tree, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"and"},{"type":"text","text":" the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu"},{"type":"text","text":" tree, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"and"},{"type":"text","text":" the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"blkio"},{"type":"text","text":" tree, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"and"},{"type":"text","text":" several others. Each hierarchy has its own mount point under "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/sys/fs/cgroup/"},{"type":"text","text":":"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"ls /sys/fs/cgroup/\n# cpu/  cpuacct/  cpuset/  memory/  blkio/  devices/\n# freezer/  net_cls/  net_prio/  perf_event/  hugetlb/  pids/"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Many directories, many mount points, many independent trees. Here''s what that fragmentation actually looks like:"}]},{"type":"interactive","attrs":{"component":"HierarchyExplorer","props":{},"hydrate":"visible"}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"With a v1 cgroupfs layout, when a container runtime creates a container, it has to:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Create a directory in "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/sys/fs/cgroup/cpu/docker/<container-id>/"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Create a directory in "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/sys/fs/cgroup/memory/docker/<container-id>/"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Create a directory in "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/sys/fs/cgroup/blkio/docker/<container-id>/"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"... repeat for every mounted controller hierarchy"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Write the process PID to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cgroup.procs"},{"type":"text","text":" in each directory"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Set the appropriate limits in each directory"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Compare that to cgroupsv2, where the same process gets one line:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":null,"title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"0::/kubepods.slice/kubepods-pod1a2b3c.slice/cri-containerd-a1b2c3d4e5f6.scope"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It''s worth pausing here, because the original v1 design was textbook good engineering. Independent trees per controller is exactly how a senior engineer would propose this from scratch today: small, decoupled subsystems, each responsible for one resource, free to evolve on its own timeline. Every Cloud Native principle we now take for granted points at that shape. The decoupling is a feature when the consumer is a sysadmin grouping arbitrary processes for arbitrary policies, limiting CPU on one grouping while throttling I/O on another."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Containers turn that assumption inside out. A container is a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"bundle"},{"type":"text","text":" that wants every controller applied to the same set of processes, atomically, with one accounting view. The flexibility v1 offered became a tax: every controller wired up separately, every limit set separately, every monitoring query stitched back together by hand. v2 is the convergence that shape demanded."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"why-it-was-a-mess"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why It Was a Mess"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The structural problem cascaded into operational pain."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Operations weren''t atomic."},{"type":"text","text":" Moving a process between cgroups meant writing its PID multiple times, across multiple "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cgroup.procs"},{"type":"text","text":" files. There was no way to say \"move this process across all controllers at once.\" If your runtime crashed between step 3 and step 4, the process might be limited on CPU and memory but not on block I/O. Container runtimes had to build their own retry-and-synchronization logic on top."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Interfaces were inconsistent."},{"type":"text","text":" CPU alone had two completely different paradigms. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.shares"},{"type":"text","text":" (values from 2 to 262144) gave you proportional weighting: a container with 1024 shares got twice the CPU time of one with 512, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"but only when both were competing"},{"type":"text","text":". If you were alone, shares didn''t matter. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.cfs_quota_us"},{"type":"text","text":" (paired with "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.cfs_period_us"},{"type":"text","text":") gave you absolute caps: quota 50000, period 100000 meant 50% of one core, hard. Two files, two mental models, same controller."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Accounting was a separate controller from control."},{"type":"text","text":" Want to know how much CPU your cgroup used? Check "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpuacct.usage"},{"type":"text","text":". A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"different controller"},{"type":"text","text":", in a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"different hierarchy"},{"type":"text","text":", with its own mount point. Want to limit it? Use "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.cfs_quota_us"},{"type":"text","text":" over in the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu"},{"type":"text","text":" controller. Nobody was happy about that decision."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Memory had two failed concepts."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.soft_limit_in_bytes"},{"type":"text","text":" was supposedly advisory, but the kernel''s reclaim behavior around it was so unpredictable that most operators treated it as decorative. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.memsw.limit_in_bytes"},{"type":"text","text":" capped memory plus swap "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"combined"},{"type":"text","text":". You couldn''t set a swap limit independently, so you did arithmetic to figure out the effective swap allowance."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"No unified view."},{"type":"text","text":" To answer \"how much total resource is this container using?\", monitoring tools had to read CPU from paths like "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/sys/fs/cgroup/cpuacct/docker/<id>/cpuacct.usage"},{"type":"text","text":", memory from "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/sys/fs/cgroup/memory/docker/<id>/memory.usage_in_bytes"},{"type":"text","text":", block I/O from "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/sys/fs/cgroup/blkio/docker/<id>/blkio.throttle.io_service_bytes"},{"type":"text","text":", and stitch the answers together by correlating paths across hierarchies. cAdvisor, Prometheus node_exporter, and every other monitoring tool had to implement this stitching. It worked. It was fragile, slow, and ugly."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(There were also smaller indignities. The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpuset"},{"type":"text","text":" controller''s "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cgroup.clone_children"},{"type":"text","text":" flag, where child cgroups didn''t inherit the parent''s CPU and memory node assignments unless you flipped it on, broke expectations everywhere else. v2 dropped the flag entirely: children of a cpuset cgroup inherit their parent''s "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpus"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"mems"},{"type":"text","text":" by default, and that''s that.)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tejun Heo saw the mess and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://lkml.org/lkml/2014/3/13/503","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"proposed a fix in early 2014"},{"type":"text","text":": a single unified hierarchy. That patchset would take two years to land and another four to stabilize."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"cgroupsv2-one-tree"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"cgroupsv2: One Tree"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tejun Heo''s rewrite kept the conceptual simplicity of \"cgroups are directories with resource knobs\" and fixed the structural mistake: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"there is exactly one hierarchy."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That decision ripples through every aspect of the design."}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# cgroupsv2: one mount point\nmount | grep cgroup\n# cgroup2 on /sys/fs/cgroup type cgroup2 (rw,nosuid,nodev,noexec,relatime)\n\n# One line in /proc/self/cgroup\ncat /proc/self/cgroup\n# 0::/user.slice/user-1000.slice/session-2.scope"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every controller lives on the same tree. When you move a process from one cgroup to another, it moves in all controllers atomically: one write to one "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cgroup.procs"},{"type":"text","text":" file. When you read resource usage, everything is in one place."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"the-no-internal-processes-rule"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The \"No Internal Processes\" Rule"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"cgroupsv2 enforces a rule that confused people initially: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"a non-root cgroup that distributes domain resources to child cgroups cannot also have processes directly in it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why? Resource accounting. If a parent cgroup has both processes and child cgroups, who gets charged for the parent''s processes? Do they compete with the children? How do you account for their usage? In v1, the answer varied by controller and was often wrong. Exactly the inconsistent half-applied behavior we just saw."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"v2 eliminates the ambiguity where it matters. Once a non-root cgroup enables domain controllers in "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cgroup.subtree_control"},{"type":"text","text":", processes need to live in leaf cgroups below it. A cgroup can still have both processes and children before it starts distributing those controllers, and the root cgroup is special-cased. Clean accounting, fewer controller-specific edge cases."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"explicit-controller-delegation"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Explicit Controller Delegation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In v1, every controller was implicitly available everywhere. In v2, parents explicitly delegate controllers to their children:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# See which controllers are available\ncat /sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.controllers\n# cpuset cpu io memory pids\n\n# Enable cpu and memory for child cgroups\necho \"+cpu +memory\" > /sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.subtree_control\n\n# Now child cgroups can use cpu and memory controllers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This creates a clean permission model. A child cgroup can only use controllers its parent delegated. Container runtimes and systemd need exactly this when granting resource control to unprivileged processes (like rootless containers) without giving them access to controllers they shouldn''t touch."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"atomic-kill"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Atomic Kill"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"v2 added a feature that v1 never had a clean answer for: killing every process in a cgroup, atomically, with one write:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"echo 1 > /sys/fs/cgroup/some-group/cgroup.kill"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The kernel walks the cgroup (and its descendants) and sends "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"SIGKILL"},{"type":"text","text":" to every process in one pass. No racing the process tree, no chasing PIDs that fork while you''re trying to enumerate them, no freezer-controller dance to pause the tree before you swing. Container runtimes use this to terminate a container cleanly; Kubernetes uses it indirectly through the runtime for pod shutdown."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(Some workloads, like databases, JVMs, and game engines, need per-thread rather than per-process control. v2 supports this via "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cgroup.type = threaded"},{"type":"text","text":", but it''s a niche we won''t dwell on here.)"}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"the-delta-controller-by-controller"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Delta, Controller by Controller"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here''s exactly what changed at the file level. Skim these tables if you''re migrating; the simulator below lets you feel how a v1 setting and its v2 equivalent translate to the actual file contents."}]},{"type":"interactive","attrs":{"component":"ResourceSimulator","props":{},"hydrate":"visible"}},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"cpu"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"CPU"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"cgroupsv1"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"cgroupsv2"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What Changed"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.shares"},{"type":"text","text":" (2 to 262144)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.weight"},{"type":"text","text":" (1 to 10000, default 100)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rescaled range, saner defaults"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.cfs_quota_us"},{"type":"text","text":" / "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.cfs_period_us"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.max"},{"type":"text","text":" ("},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"$QUOTA $PERIOD"},{"type":"text","text":")"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One file instead of two"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpuacct.usage"},{"type":"text","text":" (separate controller!)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.stat"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Accounting merged into cpu controller"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpuset.cpus"},{"type":"text","text":" (separate hierarchy)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpuset.cpus"},{"type":"text","text":" (same hierarchy)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No more separate mount point"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.weight"},{"type":"text","text":" rescaling is a quality-of-life improvement. Instead of the bizarre 2 to 262144 range, you get 1 to 10000 with a default of 100. A container with weight 200 gets twice the CPU time of one with weight 100. Simple."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"t' WHERE `id` = 'cgroups-from-chaos-to-control';
UPDATE `articles` SET `body` = `body` || 'ype":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.max"},{"type":"text","text":" combines quota and period into a single file: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"\"50000 100000\""},{"type":"text","text":" means 50 ms of CPU per 100 ms (50% of one core). Write "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"\"max 100000\""},{"type":"text","text":" for unlimited. No more reading two files and doing mental division to figure out the effective limit."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"memory"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Memory"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"cgroupsv1"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"cgroupsv2"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What Changed"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.limit_in_bytes"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.max"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hard limit, cleaner name"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.soft_limit_in_bytes"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.high"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Actually works now; applies throttling backpressure"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.memsw.limit_in_bytes"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.swap.max"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Independent swap control"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.usage_in_bytes"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.current"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cleaner name"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"n/a"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.low"},{"type":"text","text":" / "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.min"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"New: memory protection guarantees"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"n/a"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.pressure"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"New: PSI stall metrics"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The big behavioural change is "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.high"},{"type":"text","text":". In v1, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.soft_limit_in_bytes"},{"type":"text","text":" was almost useless. The kernel''s reclaim behaviour around it was unpredictable and slow. In v2, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.high"},{"type":"text","text":" actively "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"throttles allocations and triggers direct reclaim"},{"type":"text","text":" when the cgroup hits this threshold. Most workloads feel this as backpressure rather than a kill, which is exactly the point. It is not a kill-prevention guarantee, though: if a process allocates faster than the kernel can reclaim, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.max"},{"type":"text","text":" (or the global OOM killer) is still in play."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.low"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.min"},{"type":"text","text":" are entirely new concepts: memory "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"protection"},{"type":"text","text":". "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.min"},{"type":"text","text":" is a hard reclaim barrier within its "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"effective"},{"type":"text","text":" range: a child cannot protect more memory than its parents already protect, because the kernel caps the child''s effective "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.min"},{"type":"text","text":" to whatever the ancestor chain allows. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.low"},{"type":"text","text":" is best-effort protection on the same model; it shields the cgroup during system-wide reclaim, but under "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"local"},{"type":"text","text":" cgroup reclaim (the cgroup is at its own "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.high"},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.max"},{"type":"text","text":") the kernel will still reclaim from it. Together they let memory-sensitive workloads coexist with bursty ones on the same node without one starving the other."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"io"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"I/O"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"cgroupsv1"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"cgroupsv2"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What Changed"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"blkio.weight"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"io.weight"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Controller renamed entirely"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"blkio.throttle.read_bps_device"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"io.max"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unified format per device"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"n/a"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"io.latency"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"New: latency-targeted I/O control"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"n/a"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"io.pressure"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"New: PSI stall metrics"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The controller was renamed from "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"blkio"},{"type":"text","text":" to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"io"},{"type":"text","text":". The interface was cleaned up dramatically: instead of separate files for read bytes, write bytes, read IOPS, and write IOPS, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"io.max"},{"type":"text","text":" uses a unified per-device format:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":null,"title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8:0 rbps=1048576 wbps=max riops=1000 wiops=max"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Device "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"8:0"},{"type":"text","text":", read bandwidth limited to 1 MB/s, write bandwidth unlimited, read IOPS limited to 1000, write IOPS unlimited. One file, one line per device, all limits together."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"io.latency"},{"type":"text","text":" is new and powerful: instead of specifying throughput limits, you specify a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"target latency"},{"type":"text","text":" and the kernel throttles competing workloads to achieve it. This is much closer to what real applications actually care about."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"cleaned-up-naming"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cleaned-Up Naming"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"v2 standardised the verbs across all controllers:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"max"},{"type":"text","text":": hard limits (exceed triggers enforcement)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"high"},{"type":"text","text":": soft limits (exceed triggers backpressure)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"low"},{"type":"text","text":" / "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"min"},{"type":"text","text":": protection (guaranteed minimums)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"current"},{"type":"text","text":" / "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"stat"},{"type":"text","text":": usage and metrics"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Memory controller values are in bytes, not pages, so no more multiplying by the kernel''s page size to know what you wrote. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"\"max\""},{"type":"text","text":" as a string means unlimited. No more "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"_in_bytes"},{"type":"text","text":" suffixes everywhere. Consistent naming: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"<controller>.<metric>"},{"type":"text","text":" end to end."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"psi-the-feature-that-justifies-the-rewrite"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSI: The Feature That Justifies the Rewrite"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If there''s one feature that pays for cgroupsv2''s existence on its own, it''s PSI, or Pressure Stall Information."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSI itself isn''t new to cgroupsv2. It landed in Linux 4.20 (2018) and has always exposed system-wide files under "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/proc/pressure/cpu"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/proc/pressure/memory"},{"type":"text","text":", and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/proc/pressure/io"},{"type":"text","text":". What v2 adds is the same signal "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"per cgroup"},{"type":"text","text":": every cgroupv2 directory gets its own "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.pressure"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.pressure"},{"type":"text","text":", and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"io.pressure"},{"type":"text","text":" file, scoped to the processes inside it. That''s the missing piece v1 simply couldn''t produce."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"These tell you how much time tasks in the cgroup are "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"stalling"},{"type":"text","text":": waiting for a resource instead of doing useful work."}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"cat /sys/fs/cgroup/kubepods.slice/memory.pressure\n# some avg10=2.50 avg60=1.30 avg300=0.85 total=48291738\n# full avg10=0.00 avg60=0.00 avg300=0.00 total=0"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"some"},{"type":"text","text":" means at least one task is stalling. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"full"},{"type":"text","text":" means all tasks are stalling. The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"avg10"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"avg60"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"avg300"},{"type":"text","text":" numbers are percentages over 10-, 60-, and 300-second windows."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is transformative for monitoring. Instead of waiting for an OOM kill to tell you something went wrong (reactive), you can watch pressure metrics climb and take action before it happens (proactive). "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"some avg10=5.20"},{"type":"text","text":" means tasks were stalled on memory 5.2% of the last ten seconds. A yellow flag. Scale horizontally before pressure hits 20%. Throttle ingestion before I/O pressure spikes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Combined with "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.events"},{"type":"text","text":" (which counts OOM kills, reclaim activity, and high-watermark hits), you get much better visibility into the kernel''s reclaim, throttling, and OOM behaviour for a specific workload, without having to attribute system-wide signals back to a guilty cgroup yourself. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.events"},{"type":"text","text":" isn''t a one-off: most v2 controllers expose a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"<controller>.events"},{"type":"text","text":" file ("},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"pids.events"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.stat"},{"type":"text","text":" for usage and throttling counters, "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"io.stat"},{"type":"text","text":") on the same shape, so once you''ve learned to read one you''ve learned to read them all."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"kubernetes-maps-policy-onto-cgroups"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kubernetes Maps Policy Onto Cgroups"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Everything we''ve talked about so far is what Kubernetes builds on. When you set "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"resources.requests"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"resources.limits"},{"type":"text","text":" in a pod spec, the kubelet and container runtime translate those settings into cgroup configuration. Nothing mystical."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"But before we look at the mapping, two pieces of vocabulary the kubelet uses heavily: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"slices"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"scopes"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"On most modern Kubernetes Linux nodes, systemd is the host''s cgroup manager: it creates, names, and manages cgroup directories on the kernel''s behalf, and everything else (the kubelet, the container runtime) integrates with it rather than writing to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/sys/fs/cgroup/"},{"type":"text","text":" directly. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":".slice"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":".scope"},{"type":"text","text":" are simply systemd''s naming conventions for those cgroups. A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":".slice"},{"type":"text","text":" is a cgroup used to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}],"text":"organise"},{"type":"text","text":" other cgroups (an internal node in the tree). A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":".scope"},{"type":"text","text":" is a cgroup whose processes were started outside the unit itself (a leaf node containing externally-launched PIDs, like the ones a container runtime hands to systemd). That''s why cgroup paths on nodes using the systemd cgroup driver look like systemd named them. Because systemd did."}]},{"type":"interactive","attrs":{"component":"PodCgroupMapper","props":{},"hydrate":"visible"}},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"how-kubernetes-lays-out-the-cgroup-tree"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How Kubernetes Lays Out the Cgroup Tree"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"On a node using the systemd cgroup driver with cgroupsv2, the Kubernetes cgroup layout looks like this:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":null,"title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"/sys/fs/cgroup/\n└── kubepods.slice/\n    ├── kubepods-pod<uid>.slice/                    # Guaranteed pods\n    │   └── cri-containerd-<container-id>.scope\n    ├── kubepods-burstable.slice/                   # Burstable pods\n    │   └── kubepods-burstable-pod<uid>.slice/\n    │       └── cri-containerd-<container-id>.scope\n    └── kubepods-besteffort.slice/                  # BestEffort pods\n        └── kubepods-besteffort-pod<uid>.slice/\n            └── cri-containerd-<container-id>.scope"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The kubelet manages the pod and QoS cgroups, the container runtime manages the container cgroups, and systemd is the host cgroup manager they integrate with."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"qos-classes-shape-cgroups-and-oom-priority"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"QoS Classes Shape Cgroups and OOM Priority"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Kubernetes Quality of Service classes shape the cgroup layout and the kernel OOM score adjustment. Node-pressure eviction is a little more nuanced: the kubelet sorts pods first by whether their usage exceeds their requests (over-request pods go before under-request ones), then by Pod priority, then by how far usage has climbed past the request. QoS is still the useful mental model: BestEffort tends to go first, Guaranteed tends to go last."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Guaranteed"},{"type":"text","text":" pods have CPU and memory requests equal to limits for every container (or the equivalent pod-level resources, Kubernetes 1.32+). They get their own slice directly under "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"kubepods.slice"},{"type":"text","text":". The kubelet sets their OOM score adjustment to -997, making them the last to be killed by the kernel OOM killer."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Burstable"},{"type":"text","text":" pods have at least one CPU or memory request or limit, but do not meet the Guaranteed rules. They go under "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"kubepods-burstable.slice"},{"type":"text","text":". Their OOM score is adjusted based on the ratio of memory request to node capacity: more request means lower score means killed later."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"BestEffort"},{"type":"text","text":" pods have no CPU or memory requests or limits at either container or pod level. They go under "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"kubepods-besteffort.slice"},{"type":"text","text":" with an OOM score adjustment of 1000. When the node runs out of memory, these are the easiest targets."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"With the optional MemoryQoS feature on cgroupsv2 nodes, Kubernetes can also write "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.min"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.low"},{"type":"text","text":", and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.high"},{"type":"text","text":". As of Kubernetes 1.36, tiered memory reservation is still alpha and opt-in: with "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memoryReservationPolicy: TieredReservation"},{"type":"text","text":", Guaranteed pods get "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.min"},{"type":"text","text":" protection and Burstable pods get "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.low"},{"type":"text","text":" protection. With the default reservation policy, requests do not automatically become "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.min"},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.low"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"from-pod-spec-to-cgroup-files"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"From Pod Spec to Cgroup Files"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The default translation is:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"yaml","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"resources:\n  requests:\n    cpu: \"500m\"      # influences CPU shares / cpu.weight\n    memory: \"256Mi\"  # scheduler placement and eviction accounting\n  limits:\n    cpu: \"500m\"      # becomes cpu.max = \"50000 100000\" (50% of one core)\n    memory: \"256Mi\"  # becomes memory.max = 268435456 (bytes)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"CPU requests become proportional CPU scheduling weight. Kubernetes still starts from the v1-era "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.shares"},{"type":"text","text":" calculation, and the OCI runtime maps those shares to cgroupsv2 "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.weight"},{"type":"text","text":". The exact number depends on the runtime version:"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Request"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Newer runc/crun "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.weight"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Older linear conversion"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"100m"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"~17"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"~4"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1000m"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"~100"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"~39"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Linux CPU scheduler uses that weight for proportional CPU time when cores are contended. CPU limits become "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.max"},{"type":"text","text":": 500m translates to a quota of 50000 µs per 100000 µs period, which is 50% of one core. Memory limits map directly to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.max"},{"type":"text","text":" in bytes. Memory requests influence scheduling, eviction, and optional MemoryQoS protection when that feature is enabled and configured."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"the-cgroup-driver-choice"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Cgroup Driver Choice"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The kubelet has two options for managing the cgroup tree."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"cgroupfs driver:"},{"type":"text","text":" the kubelet directly creates directories and writes to cgroup files. Simple, but it creates a split-brain situation: systemd (which manages system services and their cgroups) doesn''t know about the kubelet''s cgroups, and the kubelet doesn''t know about systemd''s. Under memory pressure this can lead to instability because systemd and the kubelet make independent, potentially conflicting resource decisions."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"systemd driver:"},{"type":"text","text":" the kubelet and runtime integrate with systemd, often through transient units, and systemd manages the cgroup tree. Single source of truth. Both the kubelet and system services are visible in the same hierarchy, managed by the same process."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The paths look noticeably different. With the cgroupfs driver, the kubelet writes flat, kubelet-shaped directories:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":null,"title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"/sys/fs/cgroup/kubepods/\n├── pod<uid>/\n│   └── <container-id>/\n├── burstable/\n│   └── pod<uid>/\n│       └── <container-id>/\n└── besteffort/\n    └── pod<uid>/\n        └── <container-id>/"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"With the systemd driver, the same logical tree comes out with systemd''s "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":".slice"},{"type":"text","text":"/"},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":".scope"},{"type":"text","text":" suffixes baked into every directory name — that''s the layout shown earlier under \"How Kubernetes Lays Out the Cgroup Tree.\" Same structure, different names; tools that grep by path need to handle both."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For kubeadm clusters, Kubernetes 1.22 made "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"systemd"},{"type":"text","text":" the recommended driver and switched kubeadm''s own default to it (we all remember this migration, and not fondly; right?). The kubelet''s standalone default is still "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cgroupfs"},{"type":"text","text":", but Kubernetes recommends "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"systemd"},{"type":"text","text":" whenever systemd is the init system, and requires the kubelet and runtime to use the systemd cgroup driver for cgroupsv2 support. If you''re still on cgroupfs with systemd, you''re running against the grain."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"how-monitoring-reads-cgroups"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How Monitoring Reads Cgroups"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"When you run "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"kubectl top pods"},{"type":"text","text":", here''s the chain:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1,"type":null},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"kubectl top"},{"type":"text","text":" queries the metrics API."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"metrics-server scrapes the kubelet''s "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/metrics/resource"},{"type":"text","text":" endpoint."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The kubelet reads cgroup stats such as "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cpu.stat"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"memory.current"},{"type":"text","text":" from each container''s cgroup directory."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"cAdvisor (embedded in the kubelet) continuously scrapes the cgroup filesystem for detailed metrics."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"On cgroupsv2, cAdvisor reads from a single unified hierarchy. On v1, it had to stitch together data from multiple mount points and correlate by container ID or cgroup path. The v2 path is faster, simpler, and has fewer failure modes."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"operating-it"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Operating It"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Which version am I running?"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"bash","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Linux only. Returns \"cgroup2fs\" for v2, \"tmpfs\" for v1.\nstat -fc %T /sys/fs/cgroup/\n\n# Or look for the unified hierarchy marker\ntest -f /sys/fs/cgroup/cgroup.controllers && echo \"v2\" || echo \"v1\""}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"As of 2026, cgroupsv2 is the default on the mainstream modern Linux node images: Ubuntu 22.04+ (21.10+ upstream), Fedora 31+, RHEL 9+, Debian 11+, Flatcar Container Linux, Talos Linux, and other current container-focused distributions. Managed Kubernetes has mostly moved too: GKE defaults to v2 for 1.26+ node pools, AKS for 1.25+, and new EKS managed node groups on Kubernetes 1.30+ default to AL2023, which uses cgroup v2. Existing clusters and pinned node images can still be on v1, so check before migrating workloads."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"migration-gotchas"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Migration Gotchas"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Monitoring tools with hardcoded v1 paths."},{"type":"text","text":" Anything reading from "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/sys/fs/cgroup/memory/docker/"},{"type":"text","text":" is v1-specific and will break on v2. Most modern stacks handle both, but custom scripts and older agents may need updates."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Container runtimes."},{"type":"text","text":" containerd 1.4+ and CRI-O 1.20+ have v2 support. Docker Engine 20.10+ works with v2 when using the systemd cgroup driver. runc 1.0.0+ has full v2 support; older versions are incomplete."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"JVM applications."},{"type":"text","text":" The JVM''s cgroup-aware memory detection was rewritten for v2. JDKs older than 15, 11.0.16, or 8u372 do not detect v2 memory limits at all; they fall back to host memory and will quietly try to use far more memory than the cgroup allows. This is one of the most common \"my Java app OOMs on new nodes\" issues, and in 2026 there is no excuse for still being on those JVM versions — upgrade."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}],"text":"Hybrid mode."},{"type":"text","text":" Booting with "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"systemd.unified_cgroup_hierarchy=0"},{"type":"text","text":" (or older distro defaults) mounts the v2 unified hierarchy alongside the legacy v1 per-controller hierarchies, so processes show up in both trees at once. It works, but it''s explicitly a transition mechanism, not a destination — accounting splits across two interfaces and tools have to guess which one is authoritative. Pick one and commit."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"psi-in-practice"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"PSI in Practice"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The PSI metrics from earlier aren''t only useful for ad-hoc "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"cat"},{"type":"text","text":" commands. Kubernetes added kubelet PSI metrics as an alpha feature in 1.33, promoted them to beta in 1.34, and made them stable and enabled by default in 1.36. On cgroupsv2 nodes with PSI enabled in the kernel, the kubelet exposes node-, pod-, and container-level pressure data through the Summary API and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/metrics/cadvisor"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"ebpf"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"eBPF"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One bonus from v2''s unified hierarchy: BPF programs that attach to cgroup paths (for network filtering, socket operations, device policy) now have an unambiguous path to attach to. Cilium, Calico, and other CNI plugins that use eBPF for cgroup-aware policy get this for free."}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"where-were-going"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Where We''re Going"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"cgroupsv1 is legacy. The kernel''s authoritative cgroup documentation is now the v2 interface, Kubernetes deprecated cgroup v1 in 1.35, and new feature work targets v2. v2''s explicit controller delegation is also what makes rootless containers viable: systemd hands an unprivileged user their own subtree via a user-level "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":".slice"},{"type":"text","text":", with only the controllers the admin chose to delegate, and tools like Podman and rootless Docker drive resource limits inside it without ever touching root-owned cgroup files. What''s ahead is incremental: better NUMA-aware memory placement, finer delegation primitives for those rootless trees. But the structural story is settled."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Much like Kubernetes is YAML and controllers, all the way down; cgroups are directories and files on the surface, with kernel code doing the work of every write underneath."}]}]}' WHERE `id` = 'cgroups-from-chaos-to-control';
UPDATE `articles` SET `body` = `body` || '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If you build a seccomp profile by starting your API and hitting its health endpoint, you''ve only recorded what it needs to do those two things. When a database connection drops or a worker reloads, the application may need a syscall that never made it into the recording, and your new profile can block it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We''ll work through that problem with one API, starting with "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"RuntimeDefault"},{"type":"text","text":" and using the Security Profiles Operator to record, review, and test a custom profile. Along the way, we''ll deal with library fallbacks, verification in a distroless container, and what happens when the API reaches a new node before its profile does."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"start-with-runtimedefault"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Start with RuntimeDefault"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"On a conventional Linux container runtime, your application shares the node''s kernel, even if you''ve removed the shell and run it as a non-root user. Those are useful precautions, but the application still reaches the kernel through system calls, including any calls an attacker can make after compromising it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Seccomp lets you restrict which of those calls the application can make. If it never needs a particular syscall, blocking it removes one route into kernel behaviour that an attacker might otherwise reach. That makes seccomp a useful addition to kernel patching, restricted credentials and capabilities, and network controls."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The first step is to enable "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"RuntimeDefault"},{"type":"text","text":" in your Deployment''s Pod template, which gives you the runtime''s baseline policy while you work out whether a custom profile is worth maintaining:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"yaml","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"spec:\n  template:\n    spec:\n      securityContext:\n        seccompProfile:\n          type: RuntimeDefault"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your containers inherit this setting unless they specify their own profile, although privileged containers run unconfined. Because the runtime supplies the rules, changing the runtime or its version can also change the policy. The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/node/seccomp/","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"Kubernetes seccomp reference"},{"type":"text","text":" describes how Kubernetes selects the profile."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Keep the field explicit, because omitting it leaves the container "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"Unconfined"},{"type":"text","text":" unless the node has seccomp defaulting enabled. Setting "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"RuntimeDefault"},{"type":"text","text":" also satisfies the seccomp requirement in the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/#restricted","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"Restricted Pod Security Standard"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"decide-whether-a-custom-profile-is-worth-maintaining"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Decide whether a custom profile is worth maintaining"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The runtime''s default has to accommodate many applications, so it allows operations your API may never need. A custom profile can restrict those permissions further, but you''re also taking on the work of testing it whenever the application, its dependencies, the base image, or the runtime changes. Once you ship a profile, maintaining it becomes part of releasing the application."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That work is worth considering for workloads that accept untrusted input, share nodes with other tenants, or expose components where code execution vulnerabilities are a realistic concern. Before taking it on, though, you need tests that exercise more than startup and a health check; until you have that coverage, keep "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"RuntimeDefault"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If you''re running hostile or mutually untrusted workloads, you should also look at the isolation boundary itself. "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://gvisor.dev/docs/","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"gVisor"},{"type":"text","text":" implements an application kernel in userspace, while "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://github.com/kata-containers/kata-containers","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"Kata Containers"},{"type":"text","text":" runs workloads in lightweight virtual machines. Both can be integrated through Kubernetes RuntimeClass when the corresponding runtime handler is installed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You''ll need to check their compatibility, performance, and platform requirements against your workload, but they address an isolation decision that a shorter syscall list alone can''t resolve: how the application is separated from the host kernel."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Daniel Mangum and Sascha Grunert demonstrate applying profiles and tracing blocked calls in "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://rawkode.academy/watch/introduction-to-the-kubernetes-seccomp-operator-rtfm-with-rawkode","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"this session from 2020"},{"type":"text","text":". The operator has changed since then, so use the SPO 1.1.0 examples below for the current recording workflow."}]},{"type":"video","attrs":{"videoId":"jk9tbioqoc16ukeec1z9fi5h","thumbnailUrl":null}},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"record-the-api-where-it-runs"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Record the API where it runs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Security Profiles Operator, or SPO, handles both recording workloads and distributing profile files to nodes. These examples target "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/security-profiles-operator/releases/tag/v1.1.0","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"SPO 1.1.0"},{"type":"text","text":", with API details checked against upstream documentation and source on 1 October 2026. One detail to keep in mind as you name resources: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"ProfileRecording"},{"type":"text","text":" is namespaced, but "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"SeccompProfile"},{"type":"text","text":" is cluster-scoped, so profile names need to be unique across your application namespaces."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Use the release''s "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/security-profiles-operator/blob/v1.1.0/installation.md","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"installation guide"},{"type":"text","text":" to prepare a controlled Linux test cluster. An ordinary installation requires cert-manager, and BPF recording also needs kernel BTF at "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux"},{"type":"text","text":", along with permission to run the privileged recorder and host-PID daemon."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"With SPO installed in "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"security-profiles-operator"},{"type":"text","text":", enable the recorder:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"sh","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kubectl -n security-profiles-operator patch spod spod --type=merge \\\n  -p ''{\"spec\":{\"enricher\":{\"enableBpfRecorder\":true}}}''"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The operator adds the BPF recorder to the DaemonSet asynchronously, so check that it has appeared in the Pod template before checking the rollout. Otherwise, this command can report that the previous revision is healthy while the recorder is still being configured:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"sh","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kubectl -n security-profiles-operator rollout status daemonset/spod \\\n  --timeout=120s"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Once the rollout finishes, confirm that the recorder containers are present and ready, and inspect their startup logs on the nodes you''ll use. Missing BTF or a recorder that can''t start needs fixing before you create the application, otherwise there won''t be a recording to review."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Next, create a namespace and opt it into recording with the label that enables SPO''s recording webhook:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"sh","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kubectl create namespace seccomp-demo\nkubectl label namespace seccomp-demo spo.x-k8s.io/enable-recording=true"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Within that namespace, the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"ProfileRecording"},{"type":"text","text":" selects the Pods and containers to record. Save this as "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"recording.yaml"},{"type":"text","text":":"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"yaml","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"apiVersion: security-profiles-operator.x-k8s.io/v1\nkind: ProfileRecording\nmetadata:\n  name: seccomp-demo-api-v1\n  namespace: seccomp-demo\nspec:\n  kind: SeccompProfile\n  recorder: Bpf\n  mergeStrategy: Containers\n  disableProfileAfterRecording: true\n  containers:\n    - api\n  podSelector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: seccomp-demo-api"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This selects container "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"api"},{"type":"text","text":" in Pods labelled "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"app: seccomp-demo-api"},{"type":"text","text":". With "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"mergeStrategy: Containers"},{"type":"text","text":", SPO combines recordings from replicas by container name, while "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"disableProfileAfterRecording: true"},{"type":"text","text":" keeps the generated profile disabled so you can review it before explicitly enabling it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Apply the recording before creating the application Pods, because the webhook instruments new Pods and can''t attach to a container that''s already running:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"sh","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kubectl apply -f recording.yaml"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For the Deployment, use your API''s image and normal configuration so the recording reflects what you actually deploy. Replace the example image below with yours, preferably pinned by digest, and include its usual arguments, environment, volumes, and probes. The template assumes an image that runs as a non-root user; keep the resource and container names so the later commands match."}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"yaml","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: seccomp-demo-api\n  namespace: seccomp-demo\nspec:\n  replicas: 1\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: seccomp-demo-api\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: seccomp-demo-api\n    spec:\n      securityContext:\n        seccompProfile:\n          type: RuntimeDefault\n      containers:\n        - name: api\n          image: registry.example.com/team/api:1.0.0\n          securityContext:\n            runAsNonRoot: true\n            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n            capabilities:\n              drop: [\"ALL\"]"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Save the adapted Deployment as "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"api.yaml"},{"type":"text","text":", then start it:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"sh","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kubectl apply -f api.yaml\nkubectl -n seccomp-demo rollout status deployment/seccomp-demo-api \\\n  --timeout=120s"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The new Pod matches the recording selector and keeps "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"RuntimeDefault"},{"type":"text","text":" while the BPF recorder observes it. Check that the architecture, libraries, runtime, and configuration match production, and if the application fails under the baseline, investigate that before trying to build a custom policy around it."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"exercise-the-paths-a-health-check-misses"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Exercise the paths a health check misses"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Start the recording exercise with your integration tests, then add any operational paths they miss, including how the application recovers when something fails:"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Scenario"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Behaviour to exercise"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Startup"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Configuration loading, migrations, initial DNS and TLS connections"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Normal requests"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every supported endpoint, upload, and background job"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dependency failure"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Timeouts, connection loss, retry, and reconnection"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Filesystem activity"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Temporary files, cache writes, cleanup, and log rotation"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Process lifecycle"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Worker creation, reload, graceful shutdown, and signals"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Operational tasks"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Backups, scheduled jobs, and maintenance commands"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Supported environments"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null,"align":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every production architecture and relevant feature configuration"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Repeating one request thousands of times won''t tell you much about the paths it never touches. Spend that time exercising different behaviour, including graceful termination, so the recording captures how the application exits as well as how it starts."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Record the API, migration container, and telemetry sidecar separately, because combining their observations gives each container permissions that only another container needed. For the same reason, keep investigative commands out of the application container during recording: a shell or debugging binary can add syscalls that the application itself never uses."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Different architectures or incompatible environments also need separate recordings. SPO''s container merge retains an architecture list from a partial profile, so merging heterogeneous recordings won''t give you a portable policy."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"finish-collection-before-merging"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Finish collection before merging"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Once you''ve exercised the workload, scale the test Deployment down so Kubernetes stops creating replacement Pods:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"sh","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kubectl -n seccomp-demo scale deployment/seccomp-demo-api --replicas=0"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now inspect the partial profiles produced by the recording:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"sh","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kubectl get seccompprofiles \\\n  -l ''spo.x-k8s.io/recording-id=seccomp-demo-api-v1,spo.x-k8s.io/recording-namespace=seccomp-demo'' \\\n  -o yaml"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Graceful termination finishes each recording, but SPO still needs time to collect the results after the Pod disappears. Wait until you can see the expected partial profile for every recorded Pod and container before deleting the recording; if one is missing, check the recorder logs to find out why."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Once all partials are saved, delete the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"ProfileRecording"},{"type":"text","text":" to trigger the container merge:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"sh","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kubectl -n seccomp-demo delete profilerecording seccomp-demo-api-v1\nkubectl get seccompprofile seccomp-demo-api-v1-api -o yaml"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You should now have a merged profile named "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"seccomp-demo-api-v1-api"},{"type":"text","text":". If the merge fails, inspect the operator''s reconciliation errors before using any output; the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/security-profiles-operator/blob/v1.1.0/profiles.md#recording-based-on-ebpf-instrumentation","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"recording guide"},{"type":"text","text":" covers this collection workflow."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"To put that profile under version control, save a clean resource containing "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"apiVersion"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"kind"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"metadata.name"},{"type":"text","text":", and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"spec"},{"type":"text","text":", without status or server-generated metadata. Call the reviewed version "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"seccomp-demo-api-v1-reviewed"},{"type":"text","text":" and leave "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"spec.state: Disabled"},{"type":"text","text":" while you inspect it. Since "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"SeccompProfile"},{"type":"text","text":" is cluster-scoped, there''s no "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"metadata.namespace"},{"type":"text","text":" to include."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"review-permissions-and-error-behaviour"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review permissions and error behaviour"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"When you select "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"Localhost"},{"type":"text","text":", Kubernetes uses your profile in place of "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"RuntimeDefault"},{"type":"text","text":", so you need to compare the candidate with the actual runtime baseline before switching. That comparison includes argument restrictions: a profile with fewer syscall names can still grant wider permissions if it drops the restrictions the runtime applied."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The BPF candidate records syscall names without argument constraints. For example, if the baseline permits "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"socket"},{"type":"text","text":" only when its first argument is "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"AF_INET"},{"type":"text","text":", replacing it with an unrestricted "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"socket"},{"type":"text","text":" rule also allows other families, such as "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"AF_VSOCK"},{"type":"text","text":". Keep the argument restrictions your policy needs as you review the recording."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"There are limits to what those restrictions can express. Ordinary seccomp filters can inspect numeric arguments, but they can''t dereference a pointer to inspect a filename. If you need filesystem access control, an application or launcher can apply "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://docs.kernel.org/userspace-api/landlock.html","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"Landlock"},{"type":"text","text":" restrictions on a supported kernel, although Kubernetes'' "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"seccompProfile"},{"type":"text","text":" setting doesn''t configure them."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You also need to check what happens when a call is denied, because the error can determine what the application does next. In glibc 2.42''s "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://github.com/bminor/glibc/blob/glibc-2.42/sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/clone-internal.c#L94-L109","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"internal clone helper"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"ENOSYS"},{"type":"text","text":" from "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"clone3"},{"type":"text","text":" triggers a fallback to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"clone"},{"type":"text","text":", while "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"EPERM"},{"type":"text","text":" takes a different path. Both deny the call, but they can produce different application behaviour, and a library update can change that behaviour without any change to your own source."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If your tested application needs that fallback, a targeted rule can preserve it. This fragment belongs under "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"spec.syscalls"},{"type":"text","text":" for an amd64 or arm64 profile, where errno 38 is "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"ENOSYS"},{"type":"text","text":":"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"yaml","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"- names: [clone3]\n  action: SCMP_ACT_ERRNO\n  errnoRet: 38"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Remove "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"clone3"},{"type":"text","text":" from any existing allow rule before adding the denial, then verify that the caller falls back as expected and that the older syscall is permitted. This rule depends on the behaviour of the caller: other libraries and syscalls have different fallback conditions, so returning "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"ENOSYS"},{"type":"text","text":" for everything can change feature detection across the application."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"SPO''s resource also differs from the OCI JSON format here: OCI supports "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"defaultErrnoRet"},{"type":"text","text":", but SPO 1.1.0 exposes per-rule "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"errnoRet"},{"type":"text","text":" and has no "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"spec.defaultErrnoRet"},{"type":"text","text":" field. Use the release''s "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/security-profiles-operator/blob/v1.1.0/deploy/base-crds/crds/seccompprofile.yaml","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"SeccompProfile schema"},{"type":"text","text":" when editing the resource, and check the observed errno under your runtime, which may add compatibility handling of its own."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"install-a-logging-version-first"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Install a logging version first"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before enforcing the candidate, give yourself a way to see which calls the recording missed without blocking all of them. Copy the reviewed profile to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"observe-profile.yaml"},{"type":"text","text":", keeping its architectures and reviewed syscall rules, then change these fields:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"yaml","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Fragment of the copied SeccompProfile\nmetadata:\n  name: seccomp-demo-api-v1-observe\nspec:\n  state: Enabled\n  defaultAction: SCMP_ACT_LOG"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"With "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"SCMP_ACT_LOG"},{"type":"text","text":" as the default, calls that don''t match a rule are permitted and eligible for logging, while your allow rules continue to work as before. Any explicit denial rules still deny, so this isn''t a way to turn off the whole policy. It does broaden access for the exercise, which is why it belongs in isolated staging with test data."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For seccomp enrichment in SPO 1.1.0, configure the audit-log source:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"sh","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kubectl -n security-profiles-operator patch spod spod --type=merge \\\n  -p ''{\"spec\":{\"enricher\":{\"enableLogEnricher\":true,\"logEnricherSource\":\"Auditd\"}}}''"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Wait until the daemon template includes "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"log-enricher"},{"type":"text","text":", then check the new rollout and confirm those containers are ready:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"sh","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kubectl -n security-profiles-operator rollout status daemonset/spod \\\n  --timeout=120s"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The enricher reads auditd output from "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/var/log/audit/audit.log"},{"type":"text","text":", with a documented fallback to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/var/log/syslog"},{"type":"text","text":", so you''ll need a working audit path on the node. Check that "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"kernel.seccomp.actions_logged"},{"type":"text","text":" includes "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"log"},{"type":"text","text":" and that records actually reach that path; the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/security-profiles-operator/blob/v1.1.0/profiles.md#recording-based-on-audit-log","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"SPO logging prerequisites"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://docs.kernel.org/userspace-api/seccomp_filter.html#return-values","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"kernel action semantics"},{"type":"text","text":" explain the requirements."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Install the observation profile and read its node-relative path:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"sh","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kubectl apply -f observe-profile.yaml\nkubectl wait --for=jsonpath=''{.status.status}''=Installed \\\n  seccompprofile/seccomp-demo-api-v1-observe --timeout=120s\nkubectl get seccompprofile seccomp-demo-api-v1-observe \\\n  -o jsonpath=''{.status.localhostProfile}{\"\\n\"}''"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Once the profile reports "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"Installed"},{"type":"text","text":", the final command should return the path to its generated JSON file. Use that returned path in the Deployment, and check that the profile has been installed on every node eligible to run the application before starting it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The API container gets the custom profile, while the Pod''s "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"RuntimeDefault"},{"type":"text","text":" setting remains the default for other containers:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"yaml","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Fragment of the Deployment''s Pod template\nspec:\n  securityContext:\n    seccompProfile:\n      type: RuntimeDefault\n  containers:\n    - name: api\n      securityContext:\n        seccompProfile:\n          type: Localhost\n          localhostProfile: <path-returned-by-the-operator>"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Add the container profile to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"api.yaml"},{"type":"text","text":", preserving its other settings, set "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"replicas: 1"},{"type":"text","text":", and apply it to create a fresh API container with the observation filter. With the enricher running, repeat the workload exercise and inspect the enriched events:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"sh","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kubectl -n security-profiles-operator logs \\\n  -l name=spod -c log-enricher --since=10m --prefix=true"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This reads logs from the selected daemons, including the one on the application node, where you should look for syscall events attributed to your test workload. For each unexpected call, work out which code path needs it before adding a permission to the allowlist."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An empty log needs checking too, because it can mean missing events, attribution failures, or dropped audit records. To check the logging path, use the harmless probe later in this article with a disposable observation copy named "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"seccomp-demo-api-v1-log-check"},{"type":"text","text":", removing "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"getppid"},{"type":"text","text":" from existing rules so the "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"SCMP_ACT_LOG"},{"type":"text","text":" default handles it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Install that version, read its path, and start fresh test containers before running the probe. Once you''ve confirmed that its event arrives, return to the observation candidate. Even with a working logging path and no unexpected events, you''ll still need to rerun the scenario table under enforcement."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"enforce-the-reviewed-version-and-canary-it"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Enforce the reviewed version and canary it"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Once the logging exercise and review are complete, set "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"spec.state: Enabled"},{"type":"text","text":" in "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"reviewed-profile.yaml"},{"type":"text","text":". Keep the reviewed enforcement default action, typically "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"SCMP_ACT_ERRNO"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"sh","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kubectl apply -f reviewed-profile.yaml\nkubectl wait --for=jsonpath=''{.status.status}''=Installed \\\n  seccompprofile/seccomp-demo-api-v1-reviewed --timeout=120s\nkubectl get seccompprofile seccomp-demo-api-v1-reviewed \\\n  -o jsonpath=''{.status.localhostProfile}{\"\\n\"}''"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Use this version''s returned path in "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"api.yaml"},{"type":"text","text":" and apply the Deployment to start fresh containers, then rerun the complete exercise. This is where you check that startup, fallback paths, normal requests, graceful shutdown, and recovery after failures all work when the policy can actually block calls."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If a test fails with "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"EPERM"},{"type":"text","text":", don''t assume the nearest syscall in a log needs adding to the profile. Filesystem permissions or another security control can produce the same error, so use the logging evidence and a controlled comparison to establish which syscall and code path are involved before changing a permission."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Once those tests pass, canary the profile before rolling it out, keeping the previous version available for rollback. Updating a profile file won''t replace the filter in an existing process, so both a new version and a rollback need new containers to pick up the intended rules."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"what-happens-when-a-node-joins"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What happens when a node joins?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"Localhost"},{"type":"text","text":" profile''s file has to be on the node before the container can start. If the scheduler places the API on a newly joined node before SPO installs it, container creation fails and Kubernetes retries, commonly reporting "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"CreateContainerError"},{"type":"text","text":". There''s no automatic fallback to "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"RuntimeDefault"},{"type":"text","text":" while the file is missing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If that startup delay is acceptable, you can let Kubernetes retry while SPO installs the profile. If it isn''t, have node provisioning register new nodes with a "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"NoSchedule"},{"type":"text","text":" startup taint that the SPO daemon tolerates, then remove the taint only after every required profile reports "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"Installed"},{"type":"text","text":" for that node. A required node-affinity label maintained on the same condition is another option."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You''ll need to implement that gate in platform provisioning or a controller, since a ready SPO daemon doesn''t guarantee that the files exist, and one aggregate status check won''t protect nodes added later. The "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/security-profiles-operator/blob/v1.1.0/installation.md#constrain-spod-scheduling","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"installation guide"},{"type":"text","text":" describes the daemon scheduling configuration you''ll need for it."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"verify-a-distroless-container"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verify a distroless container"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You can check whether the application has a filter even when its image has no shell. An administrator can find the application''s host PID through the node''s container runtime and read "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"/proc/<pid>/status"},{"type":"text","text":" on the node."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-application/debug-running-pod/#example-debugging-using-ephemeral-containers","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"targeted ephemeral debug container"},{"type":"text","text":" can also inspect that process when the runtime supports joining its PID namespace, or you can use a Pod configured with a shared process namespace."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Whichever route you use, read the status for the application''s PID: "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"Seccomp: 2"},{"type":"text","text":" tells you that process is in filter mode, but doesn''t identify which rules it has. To check a particular rule''s behaviour, combine the expected profile path with a test inside the application container. The debug container has its own seccomp profile, so running a probe there tests the debugger''s rules even if it can see the application''s processes."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"id":"run-a-harmless-syscall-probe-in-the-api-container"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Run a harmless syscall probe in the API container"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Use a disposable copy of the workload and profile for this test. Save the following as "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"seccomp-probe.c"},{"type":"text","text":":"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"c","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"#include <errno.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <sys/syscall.h>\n#include <unistd.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    long parent = syscall(SYS_getppid);\n    if (parent == -1) {\n        perror(\"getppid\");\n        return 1;\n    }\n    printf(\"getppid=%ld\\n\", parent);\n    return 0;\n}"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Build it on Linux for the application''s architecture with a static C toolchain:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"sh","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"cc -static -Os -o seccomp-probe seccomp-probe.c\nkubectl -n seccomp-demo create configmap seccomp-probe \\\n  --from-file=seccomp-probe"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The binary calls "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"getppid"},{"type":"text","text":" directly and reports the result, and mounting it from the ConfigMap lets you run it in the test container without adding a shell or compiler to the image. Just keep the binary below the ConfigMap size limit."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Add these fields to the test Deployment''s Pod template, preserving the API container''s image, profile, and other settings:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"yaml","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Fragment of the Deployment''s Pod template\nspec:\n  volumes:\n    - name: seccomp-probe\n      configMap:\n        name: seccomp-probe\n        defaultMode: 0555\n  containers:\n    - name: api\n      volumeMounts:\n        - name: seccomp-probe\n          mountPath: /diagnostics\n          readOnly: true"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For the control run, replace the API container''s entire "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"seccompProfile"},{"type":"text","text":" field with "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"type: RuntimeDefault"},{"type":"text","text":", then apply the adapted Deployment and execute the binary directly:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"sh","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"kubectl apply -f api.yaml\nkubectl -n seccomp-demo rollout status deployment/seccomp-demo-api \\\n  --timeout=120s\nkubectl -n seccomp-demo exec deployment/seccomp-demo-api -c api -- \\\n  /diagnostics/seccomp-probe"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You should get a parent PID and exit status zero, confirming that the binary can run in this container. Keep the image, mount, and other security settings constant for the remaining runs so you can compare the effect of changing the profile."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Next, create a disposable copy of the reviewed candidate named "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"seccomp-demo-api-v1-probe-control"},{"type":"text","text":". Remove "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"getppid"},{"type":"text","text":" from existing rules and add this rule under "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"spec.syscalls"},{"type":"text","text":":"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"yaml","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"- names: [getppid]\n  action: SCMP_ACT_ALLOW"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Install this version, read its path, and start fresh test containers with it, then check that the probe prints a parent PID and exits successfully. That control matters because the static helper may need startup or output syscalls that the API recording missed; you need to know it works before testing whether the profile can deny "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"getppid"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If extra permissions are needed to run the helper, add them only to these validation copies. They support the test binary and shouldn''t make their way into the production policy."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Copy that working control as "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"seccomp-demo-api-v1-probe"},{"type":"text","text":" and replace only its "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"getppid"},{"type":"text","text":" rule:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"yaml","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"- names: [getppid]\n  action: SCMP_ACT_ERRNO\n  errnoRet: 1"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"On amd64 and arm64, errno 1 is "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"EPERM"},{"type":"text","text":". Install this version, wait for distribution, and use its returned path to start fresh API containers, checking that the application can start with the temporary rule before running the same probe command again."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This time, you should get "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"getppid: Operation not permitted"},{"type":"text","text":" and a non-zero exit status. Since the helper worked under the con' WHERE `id` = 'seccomp-kubernetes';
UPDATE `articles` SET `body` = `body` || 'trol profile and you''ve changed only its "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"code"}],"text":"getppid"},{"type":"text","text":" rule, the comparison tests that denial inside the application container. If the helper fails before reaching the call, you''ll need to diagnose its startup separately."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The validation profiles stay out of production; use the application''s full integration exercise to check that the reviewed profile is compatible with the API itself."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"id":"ship-the-profile-with-the-application"},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ship the profile with the application"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Store the reviewed profile alongside the application, tied to the image digest you tested, so you can tell which policy was validated for each release. Keep the architecture, runtime, node environment, and exercised scenarios with that version''s validation evidence too."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For a local Linux workflow, SPO''s "},{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/security-profiles-operator/blob/v1.1.0/cli.md","target":null,"rel":null,"class":null,"title":null}}],"text":"spoc CLI"},{"type":"text","text":" can record a command and convert the output:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","attrs":{"language":"sh","title":null,"meta":null},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"sudo spoc record -o api-candidate.yaml ./api\nspoc convert -o api-candidate.json api-candidate.yaml"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Exercise the application while it runs, then terminate it to finish the recording. The CLI adds OCI compatibility syscalls by default, so review those alongside the observed calls before testing the resulting profile with the container image and Kubernetes runtime you''ll deploy."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"When the application, libraries, base image, runtime, architecture, or relevant node environment changes, make a new recording and review the diff before accepting it. Repeat the logging and enforcement tests for that version, then roll it out under a new profile name with the previous version still available for rollback."}]}]}' WHERE `id` = 'seccomp-kubernetes';
